 "source": "Cox & D'Antonio 3e Table C.2, PDF pages 547-548 (printed pp. 490-491)",
 "about": "The twenty-two rows of Table C.2, \"3D BEM predictions random incidence\", print over two pages, sideways on the page, in the same three bold groups as Table C.1: sinusoidal cross-section, triangular cross-section, and rectangular cross-section battens. The battens group has ten rows rather than six, because the page adds a series that spaces the battens out: the same 4 cm batten on a 20 cm period with w going to zero, then 5, 10 and 15 cm. The columns are the one-third octave bands from 250 Hz to 4 kHz, and the values are correlation scattering coefficients at random incidence, dimensionless. Every cell is filled.\n\nThese numbers were computed rather than measured, by the same three-dimensional boundary element model as Table C.1, and the two tables share a source line and a warning; both are quoted in the about of that table's data file.\n\nEach group closes with a row labelled \"h/L = 35\", \"h/L = 40\" or \"h/L = 20\". As in Table C.1 the label is a percentage with the sign missing, and this table proves it twice: the triangular \"h/L = 40\" carries the same thirteen values, character for character, as its own \"h = 8 cm, L = 20 cm\", and the battens \"h/L = 20\" carries the same values as \"h = 4 cm, L = 20 cm, w = 10 cm\". The sinusoidal \"h/L = 35\" matches no row and falls between two, so it is the h = 7 cm case, which is not tabulated.\n\nOne row is printed twice on the same page. \"h = 4 cm, L = 20 cm, w = 10 cm\" closes the height series of the battens group and opens the spacing series, with identical values both times, because it is the same surface serving as the reference for two comparisons. Both rows are kept, in printed order, and the second carries the note that it is the repeat.",
